WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.010
During our first big-screen introduction to
Isla Nublar, many of us fell in love with

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:09.599
the Jurassic Park movies, but in the 20-plus
years since then, fans have uncovered many

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:11.429
mistakes and issues with the films.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.590
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm explaining how Jurassic World:

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.890
Fallen Kingdom fixes or addresses six movie
mistakes and complaints fans have about the

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.290
Jurassic movies.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.700
Spoilers ahead for the Jurassic World movies,
so take care if you haven't seen them yet.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.920
The brutal, drawn-out death of Claire's personal
assistant Zara in Jurassic World attracted

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.660
plenty of criticism.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.810
Zara's demise, which saw her being pounced
on by multiple Pteranodons and snapped up

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:41.379
by a Mosasaur, certainly seemed over the top
given all she'd done was lose track of Claire's

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:43.600
nephews after they were dumped on her.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.800
For Fallen Kingdom though, co-writer Colin
Trevorrow says he learned his lesson, and

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.190
told Empire that he "made sure that every
death was earned.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.769
Everybody deserves their death in this movie."

00:00:53.769 --> 00:01:01.819
He explained that it's "horrible people" who
massistant Eli Mills.

00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:06.520
The T-Rex goat scene in Jurassic Park is an
iconic moment and there was a brief call-back

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.530
to it in the first Jurassic World.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.420
Yet some niggling questions have been raised
about the original scene, such as given the

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.700
size of the goat, why would a T-Rex leave
a leg behind and not eat the whole animal.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.240
Well, in Fallen Kingdom when the T-Rex is
moved into Lockwood Manor's basement and she

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.310
eats the goat, this time the filmmakers specifically
showed Rexy swallowing up that final goat

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.310
leg.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.240
And there's another little nod to that Jurassic
Park goat moment when Rexy is chomping down

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.840
on Eli Mills and one of his legs flies off,
Carnotaurus catches it but then leaves it

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.780
behind after being scared off by Rexy.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.479
By the way, the T-Rex and goat scene was actually
censored in some countries like Italy and

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:48.869
I talk more about that in my video on Fallen
Kingdom's deleted and censored scenes.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:52.670
Something that irked many fans in Jurassic
World was how Claire managed to spend the

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.759
whole movie running around the island in stilettos.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:59.820
Of course, watching a dinosaur movie means
we have to suspend our disbelief, but seeing

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.490
a T-Rex outrun by Claire who was wearing high
heels that never snapped, just seemed a little

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.780
too silly and unnecessary.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.899
Bryce Dallas Howard defended her stilettos
saying they reflected how her character was

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:15.400
more at home in a corporate environment and
was disconnected from the animals in the park.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.050
Fallen Kingdom director J.A.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.290
Bayona acknowledged Jurassic World's "heel-gate"
with his first shot of Claire in Jurassic

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.540
World 2 focusing on her in heels in the office
elevator, just like the first movie.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.110
However, once Claire gets back on the island,
she ditches her heels for some sturdy boots,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.880
a much more practical choice for the sequel's
action scenes.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.209
Although it's well known that many real-life
dinosaurs had feathers, none in the first

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:40.750
Jurassic Park movie did.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.580
In fact, dinosaur consultants on that movie
did recommend that dinos such as the raptors

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.620
and the T-Rex should have feathers; however,
unfortunately, computer animation at that

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.060
time couldn't handle creating feathers realistically
so the idea was abandoned.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.390
The movie's scientific inaccuracy was acknowledged
by Jurassic Park 3's filmmakers who gave a

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.970
nod to feathered dinosaurs by adding a little
tuft of quills to the velociraptors.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.840
But when the franchise returned with Jurassic
World, a decision was made to stick with featherless

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.840
raptors.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.810
Director Colin Trevorrow defended this choice
by saying that Steven Spielberg had been unhappy

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.400
with the look and feel of feathered dinosaurs
in the Terra Nova TV series which Spielberg

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.599
executive produced between the original and
new trilogy.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:24.880
Jurassic World 2 addresses its lack of feathered
raptors by adding a little tuft of quills

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.750
to the new Indoraptor's head while still maintaining
the franchise's continuity by keeping the

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.030
overall scaly look of most of the Jurassic
dinos.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.939
By the way, the movies have an in-universe
justification for the lack of accuracy in

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:42.500
their dino designs as they aren't pure dinosaurs
as their genes have been mixed with, for example,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.780
frog DNA.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.840
And Wu explained in Jurassic World that Masrani
wasn't interested in realistic-looking dinos,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.840
just scarier ones.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.420
"If their genetic code was pure, many of them
would look quite different."

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:54.890
"But you didn't ask for reality."

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.910
"You asked for more teeth."

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:00.590
Although Jurassic Park 3 is one of the less
popular entries in the franchise, many fans

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.209
do love the Spinosaurus which was introduced
in that movie.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:09.060
And key to designing that dinosaur was Ricardo
Delgado, who contributed storyboards and concept

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.220
art for the creature.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.870
Delgado is a film and comic book artist who
went uncredited for his contributions to the

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.330
movie; however, Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom
pays homage to him with an easter egg via

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:23.949
congresswoman Delgado who Claire speaks to
on the phone to get support for her plan to

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:28.150
the save the dinosaurs from their impending
extinction on Isla Nublar.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:32.760
One big complaint that fans had about Jurassic
World was that there were too many CG dinosaurs

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.050
and not enough of the old-school animatronic
dinos they loved from the original movie.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.710
We did get an animatronic Apatosaurus scene
in Jurassic World, but Colin Trevorrow explained

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:45.900
that budget limitations meant they were only
able to write one scene for an animatronic

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:46.900
dino.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:51.100
Bu after the financial success of that movie,
the sequel had a much larger budget and it

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.750
hired Star Wars practical effects legend Neal
Scanlan to create new animatronic dinos for

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:56.750
the film.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.910
Trevorrow, who co-wrote the script, made sure
to include close-up scenes with Blue, T-Rex

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.650
and the Indoraptor.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:06.770
The scene with the injured Blue where Owen,
Zia, Franklin and Claire all interact with

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:11.470
the raptor is very realistic and the use of
animatronics gave an opportunity for the actors

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.800
to have a real physical and emotional connection
to the dino.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.120
More than ten people were under the table
operating Blue during that scene and the creature

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.710
was even able to sweat and drool.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.340
The Indoraptor's close-up scenes also used
puppeteers to get more realistic and scarier

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:27.340
shots.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:32.000
VFX was then also added on top to enhance
certain moments difficult to replicate with

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.840
puppetry, such as the blink of an eye or a
snarl.

